{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Observed during bug 941b-f049-5f29-4410's rebar-debug pass.\n\nAGENTS.md sets a target of 200-500 LOC per file and a **hard cap of 800**. The CI module-size\ngate that enforces it covers `src/rebar` only, so this file is over the cap without failing\nthe build.\n\n    932 tests/external/live_jira_dc/conftest.py\n\nIt was 917 before 941b and is 932 after (941b removed the session-start field gate and the\n`_CAPABILITY_FIELD_CONFIRM_BUDGET_S` cap, and added the provisioning evidence that explains\nwhy). So 941b did not create the condition and did not meaningfully worsen it, but it also did\nnot fix it, and splitting it was out of that bug's scope.\n\n## The binding constraint: the monkeypatch surface must not move\n\nTwo unit tests load this conftest **by path**\n(`importlib.util.spec_from_file_location`) and drive it as `harness.<name>`. Critically they\ndo not merely *read* attributes, they **rebind** them:\n\n    tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_provisioning.py:101  monkeypatch.setattr(harness, \"_request\", _fake)\n    tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_provisioning.py:104  monkeypatch.setattr(harness, \"_random_project_key\", ...)\n    tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_conftest.py:92       monkeypatch.setattr(harness, \"_request\", _fake)\n\nA patch on the conftest module object only reaches callers whose **own module globals** are\nthat same namespace. So moving a *stubbed callee* (`_request`) or any of its *callers*\n(`_create_scratch_project`, `_assert_project_capabilities`, `_leaked_harness_tokens`,\n`_sweep_leaked_harness_tokens`, `wait_for_jira_dc_ready`, `jira_dc_project`) out of conftest\nsilently un-stubs the network: the moved function resolves `_request` in the NEW module's\nglobals, which the test never patched. That fails open — the unit tests would attempt real\nHTTP instead of erroring clearly.\n\nThe two seams the original filing suggested (the PAT sweep cluster and the scratch-project\nprovisioning cluster) are therefore both **unsafe**: every member of both is either a\npatch target or a patched-through caller.\n\n## The seam that is safe\n\nThe **J11 store-copy / DC-client fixture cluster** — `dc_transport`, `dc_store_copy_repo`,\n`bound_dc_issue` — already carries its own banner comment in the file explaining it lives in\nconftest solely because two sibling suites need it. It is a genuine existing call-graph\ncluster (`bound_dc_issue` consumes `dc_store_copy_repo` and `dc_transport`; all three draw on\n`_dc_support`), and no member of it is referenced by either unit test, patched or otherwise.\nRoughly 205 LOC, so the extracted module clears the 100-LOC floor and conftest lands near 730.\n\n## Plan\n\n- Extract `dc_transport`, `dc_store_copy_repo`, and `bound_dc_issue`, with their explanatory\n  banner comment, into a sibling `tests/external/live_jira_dc/_dc_fixtures.py`.\n- **Re-export them into conftest's namespace** with a top-level\n  `from _dc_fixtures import bound_dc_issue, dc_store_copy_repo, dc_transport`, so pytest\n  still collects them from conftest exactly as today (pytest registers fixtures found as\n  attributes of the conftest module, regardless of where they were defined).\n- Resolve the package-less sibling import the same way the file already resolves\n  `_engine_path` and `jira_dc_field_readiness`: insert the conftest's own directory on\n  `sys.path` before the import, rather than relying on pytest's prepend-mode insertion.\n- Thread the base URL through the **fixture graph** rather than duplicating the `_BASE`\n  constant into the new module: add a session-scoped `jira_dc_base_url` fixture in conftest\n  and have the extracted fixtures depend on it. This avoids a second copy of the env-var\n  default drifting from the first.\n- Leave `_request`, `_field_request`, `wait_for_jira_dc_ready`, `_random_project_key`,\n  `track_issue`, `dc_request`, the PAT cluster, and the provisioning cluster **in conftest**,\n  untouched, so the monkeypatch surface above is bit-for-bit preserved.\n- Add a regression test asserting the loaded-by-path conftest still exposes the fixture\n  surface, so a future move that breaks re-export fails at the unit tier instead of 35\n  minutes into a live harness run.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `tests/external/live_jira_dc/conftest.py` is at or under 800 LOC\n- [ ] the split follows an existing call-graph seam and creates no file under 100 LOC\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_conftest.py` and `tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_provisioning.py`\n      still pass (both load the conftest by PATH, so a move must keep the loaded module's\n      surface intact)\n- [ ] every symbol the unit tests monkeypatch, and every caller that must observe those\n      patches, remains defined in conftest\n- [ ] the moved fixtures are re-exported into conftest's namespace and remain resolvable by\n      `pytest --fixtures tests/external/live_jira_dc/`\n- [ ] lint and typecheck pass"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786102278158006001,"uuid":"9d7267b1-a6bd-4d47-be24-5d894796ac45"}